<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ugandan Bush War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1981">1981</a>–<a href="/years/1986">1986</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Luwero War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ugandan Bush War was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war waged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoweri-museveni">Yoweri Museveni</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-resistance-army">National Resistance Army</a> against the second government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-obote">Milton Obote</a> and then the military regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tito-okello">Tito Okello</a>. Fighting centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luwero-triangle">Luwero Triangle</a> north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a>, where many civilians were killed. The NRA took Kampala in January 1986 and Museveni became president.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ugandan-bush-war.jpg" alt="Ugandan Bush War" width="216" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Helmut Schaar · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Obote_cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-obote">Milton Obote</a>, president since Uganda's independence in 1962, was overthrown in 1971 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a>, whose military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> was itself brought down in 1979 after the Uganda-Tanzania War. In the autumn of 1980 Amin loyalists rose in the West Nile region, and the government's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uganda-national-liberation-army">Uganda National Liberation Army</a> (UNLA) answered with destruction and looting, killing over 1,000 civilians. The elections of December 1980 were officially won by Obote's Uganda People's Congress. Several factions claimed fraud, and when Obote began a campaign of political repression, opposition groups took up arms, among them the Popular Resistance Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoweri-museveni">Yoweri Museveni</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 6 February 1981 Museveni's fighters attacked the Kabamba Military Barracks in Mubende District, taking a few guns and vehicles. Museveni, who had fought with the Mozambican Liberation Front, waged a people's war that won over many civilians around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a> who saw Obote's government as serving only the north. Obote relied on Tanzanian troops, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a> gradually withdrew, and on advisors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> and other countries. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luwero-triangle">Luwero Triangle</a> the UNLA under its chief of staff, David Oyite-Ojok, waged a counter-insurgency campaign in which thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baganda">Baganda</a> civilians were killed.</p><p>Oyite-Ojok died in a plane crash in December 1983, and the army began to unravel as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acholi">Acholi</a> soldiers came to believe that Obote used them as cannon-fodder while filling the leadership with Langi. In August 1985 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-resistance-army">National Resistance Army</a> opened offensives that took Masaka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbarara">Mbarara</a>, growing to about 10,000 troops by December. A peace agreement with the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tito-okello">Tito Okello</a> broke down almost at once. The NRA captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a> on 26 January 1986, and Museveni was sworn in on 29 January.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Estimates of the dead across Uganda range from about 100,000 to 500,000, combatants and civilians together. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-committee-of-the-red-cross">International Committee of the Red Cross</a> estimated that by July 1985 Obote's government had been responsible for more than 300,000 civilian deaths, and the UNLA forcibly moved 750,000 civilians of the Luweero District into military camps. The NRA also committed atrocities: it used land mines against civilians, had many child soldiers and in the early years executed some government-aligned chiefs. Obote and Okello went into exile, and anti-NRA factions went on fighting Museveni's government in the following decades.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ugandan Bush War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Bush War</b> of 1981–1986 between Obote’s Uganda National Liberation Army and Museveni’s National Resistance Army devastated the Luwero Triangle north of Kampala.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uganda">Uganda · Military</a></li></ul></section>
